clk: mediatek: mt6797: model the Cortex-A72 cluster's clock and mux

The A72s have run at a frequency this driver picks and holds, because Linux
had no way to describe their clock. This gives it one, and the reason it goes
in the MCUMIXED driver rather than in the driver that powers the cluster up is
that half of it is already here.

WHAT THE HARDWARE ACTUALLY IS. The cluster's mux is ARMPLLDIV_MUXSEL[1:0], in
this block, in the same register as the two A53 clusters' fields. Its PLL is
NOT in this block: the vendor sets MT_CPU_DVFS_B's armpll_addr to NULL and
changes that cluster's frequency by asking the iDVFS co-processor through ATF
(BIGIDVFSPLLSETFREQ). So this adds two clocks -- "mcu_b_idvfs", whose
->set_rate is an SMC, and "mcu_b_sel", an ordinary mux -- and mediatek-cpufreq
needs nothing else to drive the cluster.

IT ALSO FIXES TWO THINGS THE OPEN-CODED VERSION WAS DOING.

mt6797-a72.c did an unlocked read-modify-write of ARMPLLDIV_MUXSEL while
mediatek-cpufreq wrote the A53 fields of the same register under
mt6797_mcumixed_lock. One shared lock is the point of moving it.

Worse, it bracketed each change with

	writel(misc | (3u << 4), topckgen + CLK_MISC_CFG_0);   ... and back

which enables and then DISABLES the armpll_div_pll1/pll2 gates by hand. Those
are the "intermediate" clock the A53 policies park on, held enabled through
the clock framework for the life of the boot. The A72's one raise per boot
therefore left armpll_div_pll1 with a framework enable count of 2 and its
hardware bit clear -- measured on build #99:

    CLK_MISC_CFG_0 = 0x01ff0000
    armpll_div_pll1   2   2   0   1092000000   ...   N

The A53 clusters have been parking on a clock the framework believes is
enabled and the register says is not, on every DVFS transition, since the
first build that onlined an A72. Nothing here re-enables it; the write simply
stops happening, and the gate stays where its consumers put it.

THE ONE PLACE THIS DRIVER DOES NOT REPORT THE REGISTER. mcu_b_sel needs its
own ->get_parent, because the framework reads a mux's parent exactly once, at
registration, and caches it. At registration the cluster is unpowered and
MUXSEL[1:0] reads 0. Selector 0 is the 26 MHz oscillator, but on this field it
does not mean "someone chose 26 MHz", it means "this cluster is off" -- the
cluster's own PLL does not exist until the firmware powers the domain holding
it, and the firmware moves the field to 1 during CPU_ON without telling
anyone (measured: MUXSEL 0x54 before, 0x55 after). Left alone, the cached
answer stays clk26m for ever and mediatek-cpufreq's first act --
clk_get_parent() then clk_set_rate() on the result -- would try to retune the
crystal. Reporting 0 as ARMPLL is the difference between the field's value and
its meaning. It cannot be corrected from outside this driver either:
clk_set_parent() to the parent the framework already believes in returns
without touching anything.

CSPM is ioremapped by address rather than taken from a DT resource. It is not
part of MCUMIXED -- it is the SoC's shared hardware-semaphore page, which ATF
takes around its own ARMPLLDIV accesses and polls for without a bound -- and
mt6797-a72.c maps it the same way for the same reason. Putting it in this
node's reg would claim it belongs to this block.

